war what is it good for our experiences  of recent years of left us very wary of  foreign interventions but do we  sometimes have a moral responsibility to  intervene or is the moral course for us  to stand by and out of conflict or is  the morality irrelevant in all of these  decisions  I think there is a risk that we draw the  conclusion from the disastrous  consequences of some recent  interventions that we therefore decide  that no such intervention can ever work  and I think that would be an erroneous  conclusion to draw I think there are  circumstances in which it makes sense  because this is unfortunately the only  resort to intervene with some form of  military equipment and yet we are in a  position now where that argument is very  difficult to prosecute because we are in  the shadow of such horrendous examples  so to draw an overarching conclusion  from those examples where I think be in  error and when I say that I don't think  we're just flying blind we've got a huge  scholarly tradition behind us which can  guide us in trying to assess the  circumstances for intervention or not  the Just War tradition is deep and  scholarly and has three two parts  firstly there must be a Just Cause and  secondly war must be conducted according  to just criteria there's also a movement  to add a third part which is justice in  the post-war reconstruction but that's  not the ancient discipline it starts  with Cicero but one of the day offices  it goes through the central Olga's teens  and comes to a real fruition with Thomas  Aquinas and there is a very strong  argument that there has to be just a  purpose and we can argue about what that  entails and secondly then the conduct of  the war must itself follow arguments of  procedural justice and the case against  interventions like Iraq is that it could  not follow those precepts there is  perfectly good reason why those that  intervention did not work if you look at  Tony Blair's Chicago speech which is  essentially attempt to update Aquinas it  wasn't advertised as such but that's in  effect what it does the fifth criteria  of intervention in that speech is that  the intervention should have a  reasonable hope of practical effect  absolutely perfectly reasonable to say  there was no prospect of that  in that in the case of that intervention  therefore it should have fallen foul of  his own precepts and the there's a  brilliant remark somebody once made of  neoconservatism is there the problem  with it is it's not conservative enough  and the Just War theory is a very  conservative thesis but it does leave  open the prospect that if there is no  other option in the case for example of  a genocide or a war to the last for  civilization that military involvement  has to be kept in reserve for fear that  there is nothing else if we get to the  position where we don't allow that in  any circumstances at all then we are  potentially at times in peril  I don't think we can really say we're  reluctant to intervene when this country  is involved in a whole number of  interventions and has been over recent  years if you look in the last 15 years  which is where we've had the war on  terror or the it changes its name but  that it was started off as the war on  terror maybe it changed its name because  it was clearly not doing what it sought  to sort to achieve but if you look at  those interventions I would argue in  Afghanistan in Iraq in Libya where we're  now talking about going back into war  again that all of these have actually  made the situation worse that the people  of Afghanistan are fleeing as refugees  from war which is still continuing after  all those years as all the people of  Iraq and Syria so it seems to me I'm not  a pacifist I'm not somebody who says  totally that war is wrong I was brought  up by parents who were involved in the  Second World War and who suffered in the  Second World War in all sorts of ways so  I and I know that people sometimes have  very strong motivations for going to war  but there has to be in modern time there  has to be a reason for doing it which  isn't just about the strategic interest  of the West and isn't just about  supporting some governments and not  others if you look at the consequences  of war they are absolutely appalling and  therefore there has to be a very very  high bar that needs to be crossed before  you can possibly do it if you look at  the refugees today if you look at the  people in Fallujah today and will ever  sign you are all know for this these  people in Fallujah has suffered now  since 2004 with war occupation siege and  are still suffering as a consequence I  believe that the growth of Isis actually  developed as part of the occupation of  Iraq if you look at where the Isis  leaders came from they met in a prison  in in southern Iraq under the under the  occupation and I hesitate to bring up  the Chilcott report which is going to  report in a month after all these years  but if you look at what Eliza Manning  Buller the head of mi5 said for example  she told the government that it would  make the threat of terror  much much greater and this is the  situation we're in I also think when  we're talking about morality of all we  should also talk about the morality of  people who oppose Wars and today of all  days when we I hope all of us mourning  the death of Muhammad Ali we should  remember that he was one of a generation  who who fought and sacrificed to oppose  war in Vietnam now I was a teenager in  those years and I remember how much  everybody told us that war was justified  how much it was about fighting communism  how much the people of Vietnam would  appreciate the liberation we know now I  don't know how the other panelists feel  about Vietnam but probably few people  now would defend that war as a just war  or as a war that we should be involved  in so let's look at the people who stand  up against war and who say these things  are wrong who have a moral opposition to  it and who sacrifice very very often in  my view the world would be a much better  place if they were listened to rather  than to the government's and the  military who take us into so many wars  we've created for ourselves a false  dichotomy I mean we've heard about two  wars both which absolutely were  contested at the moment of their  initiation and through their conduct  Iraq and Vietnam and what I think in my  view we've done we've created a on the  one hand the idea that war should  unequivocally be necessary and the polar  opposite of that is always presented as  a war of choice and this seems to me a  totally unhelpful way of looking at  these problems the war of necessity  takes us back to the idea of the second  world war a war where it would seem we  had no choice where there was an  existential conflict and it's from that  that we have a UN Security Council sorry  we have a UN Charter which says you know  the only war that is genuinely  legitimate is the war of national  self-defense and the paradox in that is  that the only war that a state is  individually entitled to engage in in  terms of international law is  essentially a very very big one you know  there's no option there for a more  limited war which is the sort of  conflicts we're talking about there is  no no capacity for what we've come to  call a war of choice but that is exactly  it seems to me where we trip ourselves  up because as is absolutely rightly been  said the barrier for going to war needs  to be very high he needs to be seen to  be unequivocally necessary for it to be  warranted to take up arms whether that's  a moral necessity or a national interest  necessity either way there needs to be  an unequivocal necessity and once we  once we therefore have made that  decision then we need to recognize that  the commitment is equivalent to being a  to Neptune to necessity clouds of its  mind I perhaps hesitate to mention you  had situation Mohammed Ali I should  hesitate to mention clouds it's when  we're talking about war but clouds of  it's argued that war actually begins  with defense and not with attack if you  invade another country then actually the  other side can simply accept that  invasion there is no necessary  resistance there's no necessary response  so the paradox is that the the apparent  aggressor is not the cause of war it is  actually the defender that is the  of war and this isn't just splitting  splitting words because the choice in  other words lies as much in the  necessity the decision to fight as it  lies in the decision to initiate the war  in the first place so so my case  essentially is that we should rid  ourselves of this distinction and simply  recognize that war is very serious  business the challenge we now have  following out of the Chicago speech of  1999 is that the UN has adopted  alongside that principle of national  self-defense a wider principle which is  the responsibility to protect it cannot  be implemented without a UN Security  Council resolution which is why it  hasn't been implemented in the case of  the Syrians and the faith that they are  all undergoing but it does seem to be  extraordinary in the current context  that we've become so bound up in the  consequences of our own Wars in  particularly of Iraq and Afghanistan  that we find ourselves inhibited in the  ways in which we behave when there is a  genuinely humanitarian arguments from  profoundly pressing moral arguments for  engagement  [Applause]
 

